Split out of #56, whose stated scope -- move, edit and delete -- is fixed.

Two write paths were left outside that fix and both are the same bug class:

- Cmd/Archive.pm sets the status to archived and saves, with no claim check at
  all. It is a third door into a status change next to move and edit --status,
  which #55 just spent its whole diff collapsing into one guarded path. archived
  has no require_claim, so #55 does not apply, but the claim takeover does: it
  can archive a task another agent is holding.
- Cmd/Handoff.pm carries its own copy of the claim rule rather than calling
  Role::ClaimTimeout::check_claim, and it is still an unguarded read-then-write.
  The lane that fixed #56 deliberately made check_claim emit a byte-identical
  message so adopting it is a zero-user-visible-change edit; it left the file
  alone only to avoid a merge conflict.

Work: route both through the shared path. Role::TaskMutation::update_task_guarded
and apply_status_change already exist and already do the compare-and-swap, so
this is adoption, not new mechanism. Handoff losing its private copy of the rule
is the point -- a rule that exists twice drifts, which is how #55 happened.

Check while there: Role::BoardAccess::save_task is now the single door for task
writes (it takes an optional expected OID and becomes the CAS). Anything that
still calls store->save_task_cas directly is another bypass waiting to happen.
Fixed and merged into main (228fffa); full suite green at 98 files / 670 tests.

#61: the exit-code half of my earlier note was my own measurement error (see the
correction above) -- the real defect was the loop dying on the first bad id and
the resulting order-dependence. Contract now: per-id runtime failures warn,
continue, and exit 1 with an "N of M ids failed" summary; a usage error aborts
the batch at exit 2 with nothing written, because it is wrong for every id at
once and collecting it would demote a 2 to a 1.

#97: archive is on the guarded path; handoff had already adopted check_claim in
an earlier merge, but its read-then-write was still unguarded and is now a
compare-and-swap. Verified afterwards that store->save_task_cas has exactly one
caller, Role::BoardAccess::save_task -- no command reaches around the door.
